@@ family_halts
- **`<build>_halts`**: on every input on which the build is never refused memory for exceeding 16 GiB (a premise: an input that needs more is not covered), and when the environment refuses nothing it is not forced to, every execution exits with $exits, without crashing, failing an assertion, hanging or tripping its sanitizer.
@@ family_halts_may_fail
- **`<build>_halts_may_fail`**: with no memory premise, and even if any request is refused at any point, every execution exits, with $exits. Stated for the plain and UBSan builds only.
@@ family_agreement
- **`<build>_agrees`**: on every input on which the plain build is never refused memory for exceeding its budget (a premise), neither build reaches an error, and the two have the same set of observable behaviours: every sequence of writes and exit status one can show, the other can too. A sanitizer report aborts, so this rules out every error the sanitizer build's configuration detects. It does not rule out a run that never ends; only a halts statement does.
@@ family_agreement_rust
- **`<build>_agrees`**: on every input on which the plain build is never refused memory for exceeding its budget (a premise), neither build reaches an error, and the two have the same set of observable behaviours: every sequence of writes and exit status one can show, the other can too. An ASan report aborts and a failed UBSan check in C code traps, both errors; a failed rustc UB check is not an error: it exits with status 79 after a line on standard error that the plain build does not write at that point. So this rules out every error the sanitizer build's configuration detects, and every failed UB check. It does not rule out a run that never ends; only a halts statement does.
@@ family_bound
- **`<build>_disagrees_at_most b`**: for inputs drawn from the harness's `Distribution.v`, the plain and sanitizer builds disagree with probability at most *b*.
@@ family_decidability
- **`<build>_agreement_decidable`**: some riscv64 program decides exactly the finite inputs on which the two builds agree. A program that does so, with a bound on how often it rejects, is a route to a disagreement bound (docs/statements.md).

